# Codex Browser QA Run: Manual Booking (40 Scenarios)

## Purpose

This file is an execution brief for Codex. Use a real interactive browser session to run the scenarios below through the admin UI, then report the defects and coverage gaps found. Do not treat this as a checklist for the user to perform and do not stop after describing a plan.

## Instructions for Codex

1. Use the available browser-control skill/tool to open the local admin app and interact with visible UI controls. Prefer the browser over direct API calls or database edits. If a scenario cannot be completed through the browser, mark it **BLOCKED** and explain why; do not silently replace it with source inspection or an API-only test.
2. Confirm the app is the local test instance at `http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/login.html` and sign in using an available local owner account. If the app or database is unavailable, record the exact blocker before attempting setup.
3. The local database was reset for this test: bookings/customers and linked transactional history are empty. Services, packages, active add-ons, checklist templates, rental items, users, and settings remain. Do not reset again, delete records, or change core catalog values during the run.
4. Use only fictional values from this file. Keep records created by the run so evidence can be inspected afterward. Restore temporary catalog changes immediately and verify the restoration.
5. Run scenarios in numerical order because some cases intentionally reuse earlier customers or create overlapping bookings. Do not repeat a case automatically after a successful write unless the scenario explicitly tests retry/idempotency.
6. For every scenario record **PASS**, **FAIL**, **BLOCKED**, or **NOT RUN**. A pass requires checking the saved result after reopening/refreshing the screen, not only seeing a success toast.
7. For each failure, capture a browser screenshot if supported and record the scenario number, URL, booking reference, exact steps, expected result, actual result, visible error, and whether it reproduced. Report severity and avoid exposing credentials or session tokens.
8. At the end, report a summary by status, then an issue list and a separate list of untested gaps. Distinguish browser-observed behavior from source/test evidence. Do not claim the flow is fully tested if any case is blocked or not run.
9. This is an audit run. Do not change application code while executing these cases. Report proposed fixes separately and wait for the user before implementing them.

## Test environment

Local admin URL: `http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/login.html`  
Database: local `akad_rentals` only  
Starting state: no bookings or customers; core services and packages are retained.

If this run is resumed later, verify the starting state first. The scenarios create records and are not safe to rerun on top of an existing run without a deliberate local reset.

Create a new customer for each scenario unless it says to reuse one. All names, phone numbers, addresses, and bookings below are fictional test data.

For scenarios that test the post-event workflow immediately, use **October 18, 2026** with an event time that has already ended. The booking form accepts past event dates in this test setup; after confirming, refresh the bookings list so the due booking can move to **Awaiting Completion**. For other scenarios, use the suggested future dates. If you change dates, keep each scenario's bookings on separate dates unless the steps explicitly require an overlap.

